        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the NordicTrack C 700 worth buying?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              For walkers and light joggers who want a genuine NordicTrack frame without paying for a touchscreen they won't use, yes, at $599-$799. It's built for walking-focused use, not for daily running or a household putting heavy weekly mileage on it, and because NordicTrack's own site no longer lists the C-Series, check the listing and warranty before you buy.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the NordicTrack C 700 still sold?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              Not on NordicTrack's own site: on October 13, 2026 it listed seven treadmills and no C-Series model. It is also no longer sold new on Amazon US. A C 700 for sale today is likely retailer or older stock, so confirm the model number and who honors the warranty. NordicTrack's current entry machines are the T Series.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the NordicTrack C 700 have a touchscreen?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              No. The C 700 uses a basic backlit LED console showing speed, incline, time, distance, and calories. It doesn't have the rotating HD touchscreens found on NordicTrack's Commercial series, which is a major part of why it costs hundreds of dollars less.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you run on the NordicTrack C 700, or is it just for walking?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              You can jog on it, but the 2.75 CHP motor and 20 x 55-inch deck are undersized for sustained running compared to NordicTrack's Commercial line. We'd call this a walking-and-light-jogging treadmill rather than a running treadmill, and shoppers who run several times a week should budget for a machine with a 60-inch deck instead.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How does the C 700 compare to the NordicTrack C 990?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              The C 990 adds a 10-inch touchscreen, a slightly larger 20 x 60-inch deck, and a modestly stronger 3.0 CHP motor for roughly $300 more. If you know you want the iFit touchscreen experience, the C 990 is worth the jump; if you just want a reliable machine for walking, the C 700 covers the same core ground for less.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need an iFit subscription to use the C 700?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              No. The C 700's console and built-in workout programs work without a paid iFit membership, and the included 30-day trial is optional. Expect setup to prompt you towards an iFit account or trial, as connected NordicTrack consoles commonly do; a free account and a paid membership are different things.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does the NordicTrack C 700 last?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              A realistic expectation is roughly 5 to 8 years under regular home walking use, assuming basic belt maintenance is kept up. That is an estimate based on its motor rating and build class, not a measured figure, and it's shorter than what we'd expect from the heavier Commercial-series frames, which is a fair trade-off given the price gap.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Common Mistakes -->
      <div class="mt-16">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buyers Make</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most of the disappointment we see with the C 700 comes from buying it for the wrong job rather than from the machine itself. These four mistakes account for nearly every complaint we found in owner reports.</p>
        <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
          <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying it as a running treadmill</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The 2.75 CHP motor and 20 x 55-inch deck are sized for walking and light jogging. Run on it four or five times a week and the 55-inch deck leaves little room at full stride while the motor works near the top of its intended range. If you run regularly, put the extra money toward a C 1650 or Commercial 1750 rather than replacing this machine in two years.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing peak horsepower against continuous duty</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Budget listings often quote a peak figure that the motor can only hold for seconds. The C 700's 2.75 rating is continuous duty, which is the number that matters. Compare like for like, or a cheaper rival with a bigger peak number will look stronger than it is.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming iFit is compulsory</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The console works without a paid membership: manual speed, manual incline, and the built-in programs all run after the 30-day trial ends. Buyers who assume they are locked into a monthly fee often overpay for a rival machine to avoid one that was never required.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping belt maintenance on a budget deck</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A lighter deck is less forgiving of a dry or misaligned belt than a commercial frame. Check belt tracking after the first 20 miles and re-lubricate on the schedule in the manual. Owners who skip this are the ones reporting slipping and motor strain inside three years.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
